== a space in the match quotes it:
'spacey file.txt'
== a plain match stays unquoted:
plainfile.txt
== a dollar in the match quotes it:
'dollar$x.txt'
== inside a single quote the space match completes bare within it:
'spacey file.txt
== inside a double quote the space match completes bare within it:
"spacey file.txt
== glob-like bytes stay literal inside open quotes:
'[C] alpha/
'star* alpha/
'question? alpha/
"[C] alpha/
"star* alpha/
"question? alpha/
== an open quote after a directory keeps the full path prefix:
outer/"[C] alpha/
== an open quote inside a command keeps the prefix:
e"cho
== a closed quote inside a command keeps the prefix:
e"cho"
== a fuzzy prefix before an open quote maps into the candidate:
foo_b"ar_baz
== open quotes escape bytes that remain active in their quote mode:
'single'\''quote.txt
"double\$\"tick\`\\name.txt
== an adjacent open double quote keeps variable expansion active:
$QUOTED_COMPLETION_UNIQUE
== a closed quoted directory continues through an unquoted slash:
'PATH'/'space file.txt'
'PATH'/plain.txt
'PATH'/star-one.txt
'PATH'/tool
== a partial basename follows a closed quoted directory:
'PATH'/plain.txt
== a quoted directory that contains a space keeps its spelling:
'space dir'/mixed.txt
== adjacent quoted and unquoted directory bytes are decoded together:
P'A'TH/plain.txt
== an escaped directory keeps its spelling:
space\ dir/mixed.txt
== a double-quoted variable directory remains active:
"$HOME"/home.txt
"$HOME"/space\ file.txt
== a single-quoted variable directory remains literal:
'$HOME'/literal-variable.txt
== an open single-quoted variable directory remains literal:
'$HOME/literal-variable.txt
== an open double-quoted variable directory remains active:
"$HOME/home.txt
"$HOME/space file.txt
== an active tilde directory expands:
~/'space file.txt'
~/home.txt
== an active tilde stays outside a quoted match:
~/'space file.txt'
== an active variable uses escapes for a matched space:
$HOME/space\ file.txt
== a quoted tilde directory remains literal:
'~'/literal-tilde.txt
== quoted and escaped tilde usernames remain literal:
~"root"/literal-tilde.txt
~r\oot/literal-tilde.txt
== quoted and escaped bytes stop an unbraced variable name:
$HO"ME"/literal-variable.txt
$HO\ME/literal-variable.txt
== a quoted glob remains literal:
== an unquoted glob after a quoted directory expands:
'PATH'/plain.txt 'PATH'/'space file.txt' 'PATH'/star-one.txt
== command position keeps only runnable files:
'PATH'/tool
